From 6fba583c74659a80b161ba0dcfc7a989140585df Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 18 Jun 2026 11:54:37 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 02567/11362] [outer-loop] multi-object push state: adaptive
 key-granularity (no regression); ka59 doubly-blocked

Goal was to unlock ka59 (push) by adding the pushed block's position to the exploration state key.
Building it surfaced a real tension and a second blocker, both now precisely diagnosed:

1. TENSION (caught a regression before shipping): tracking the block's position is the right CONCEPT,
   but a naive "track all piece-sized objects" key OVER-FRAGMENTS navigation games -- ar25 has many
   moving objects, so the multi-object key blew up its state space and BROKE its previously-working
   solve. Navigation games need a MINIMAL key (agent position); push games need the block too. The
   right granularity is genuinely game-dependent.

2. FIX -- ADAPTIVE granularity: try the minimal agent-position key first; only ESCALATE to the
   multi-object pieces key if the minimal search dries up. RESULT (no regression, all first-contact,
   no banked solve): sp80/cn04/ar25 solve on the AGENT key (4/14/15 actions); ka59 escalates to the
   pieces key.

3. ka59's SECOND blocker (discovered mid-build): its win sequence is [4,4,4,3,2,3,3,3,C:1,1,4] (per
   ops/arc_solve_registry.yaml) -- it includes a CLICK (C:1), but the explorer is keyboard-only. So
   ka59 is DOUBLY blocked: it needs multi-object state (now available via escalation) AND
   click-capable exploration. The state-key escalation alone cannot unlock it.

NET: first-contact solves hold at 3/4 (sp80, cn04, ar25), no regression; the multi-object machinery
(identify_movers, pieces-key, adaptive escalation) is built and correct; the push/click class (ka59)
is now precisely characterized as needing click-capable exploration on top of multi-object state --
a separate, named gap rather than a single tweak. Adversarial-verify: clean.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
